# Cargo's build directory. Nothing in it is authored — fingerprints, object
# files, incremental state and dependency builds, all reproducible from the
# sources by `cargo build`.
#
# 402 of these were committed in "Initial project creation." and never
# touched again, which made build output 72% of the repository's tracked
# files and left ~2700 more of them untracked in every `git status`. They
# are ignored and untracked as of 2026-09-19; the files themselves stay on
# disk, and `git rm -r --cached target` is what took them out of the index.
/target

# The review loop's captured frames live under it, at target/review-frames/:
# the PNGs stage 5 looks at plus each frame's declared cell grid. Covered by
# the rule above, named here because `mjolnir-review` keeps only the last
# three runs and a reader looking for an older one should find out where it
# went.

# Written by Mjolnir itself: answering "for this project" at a permission
# prompt appends the grant here, so it's whatever this developer approved.
# The other .mjolnir/ files are hand-authored and stay tracked.
/.mjolnir/permissions.yaml

# Private key material, which must never be committed under any name.
#
# `cosign generate-key-pair` writes `cosign.key` into the current directory,
# and the obvious place to run it is the repository root — right beside the
# `cosign.pub` that *is* meant to be committed. One `git add -A` separates
# the correct outcome from publishing the key that signs every release to a
# repository other people can read.
#
# The generated `cosign.key` is passphrase-encrypted, so a leak is not
# instantly fatal, but it is still a rotation event: new keypair, new secret,
# new public key committed, and every prior signature now verifiable only
# with a key you have repudiated.
#
# Belt and braces — the README tells you to generate the pair in a temporary
# directory and move only the public half in, so this rule should never fire.
# It exists for the day someone does it the obvious way instead.
cosign.key
*.key
*.pem

# `scripts/release.sh` stages builds and archives here. Everything in it is
# reproducible from a tag by running that script, which is the whole point of
# it — there is nothing to keep.
/dist
